    "lawCode": "Amd §444-e, RP L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4122\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the real property law, in relation to home inspection\nprofessional licensing\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to update training requirements for home\ninspectors.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Paragraph (c) of subdivision 1 of section 414 e of the real\nproperty law, as amended by section 59 of part D of chapter 60 of the\nlaws of 2012 is amended.\n\nSection 2: Provides for this act's effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill, supported by the New York State Association of Home Inspec-\ntors, will increase the required training to become a licensed home\ninspector. This increase will strengthen training for new home inspec-\ntors and ensure more effective inspections. The present 140 hours of\ninitial training for licensure are insufficient to ensure an adequate\nunderstanding of the significance, condition, and failure modes of the\nnumerous electrical, mechanical, and structural systems within a home,\nresulting in significant missed or misinterpreted conditions during home\ninspections. This increase in hours will ensure that new home inspectors\nare trained to the highest standard and fully equipped to protect home\nbuyers.\n\nThe bill also asks the Secretary of State to review and establish rules\nand regulations regarding the manner and number of hours required for\nunpaid field-based inspections as part of the training.  The 40 hour\non-site training portion of the existing initial training requirements\ndoes not allow enough time for trainees to learn to successfully identi-\nfy essential components and to properly explain the relative signif-\nicance of observed deficiencies to clients. Modern residential\nconstruction and remodeling are constantly undergoing major changes in\nmethods and materials, as well as energy conservation measures and tech-\nniques, requiring significant continuing education and training.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nFormerly A-215-a/S649-a of 2018, passed the Assembly and died in Senate\nRules.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis act has no fiscal implications to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThe act shall take effect immediately and shall apply to applications\nfor a license as a home inspector received on or after such date.\n",
